WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
The data plan: test set first.

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:07.427
Order your data work so contamination can’t happen, rather than checking for it afterwards.

3
00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:09.691
Pick and freeze the test set first.

4
00:00:09.971 --> 00:00:15.987
Select source documents (whole meetings) for the test split, label them carefully, and compute the fingerprint.

5
00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:23.627
Aim for at least 100 to 150 cases; fewer and your intervals will be too wide to support most decisions.

6
00:00:23.907 --> 00:00:26.957
Commit the file and never edit it during the project.

7
00:00:27.237 --> 00:00:33.104
If you find a label error, record it and fix it in a version 2 used for a clearly labeled rerun.

8
00:00:33.384 --> 00:00:40.701
Set aside a dev split, also from separate meetings, for tuning prompts (including the frontier model’s) and training choices.

9
00:00:40.981 --> 00:00:53.397
Build the training set only from the remaining meetings: real labeled examples, plus synthetic or distilled examples if you need volume, each checked with your Studio 1 verifier and a human spot-check.

10
00:00:53.677 --> 00:00:59.160
Run a similarity check between training inputs and test inputs as a backstop, and report it.

11
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.509
Then plan the labeling effort honestly.

12
00:01:01.789 --> 00:01:10.429
Labeling 150 test cases with four fields and evidence takes hours, not minutes, and it is the most valuable time you will spend.

13
00:01:10.709 --> 00:01:19.029
If someone else can label or check a sample, measure your agreement; the agreement rate is a ceiling on how precisely any system can be measured.
